+## Platform and collection interpretation
+
+Infer platform, release, host role and collection scope from the supplied records. Apply
+Windows, Linux and macOS expectations only to the hosts and sources that support them.
+A network sensor observing a Mac does not make its rows native Mac endpoint telemetry.
+Do not require a particular product, audit facility, event family or filesystem artifact
+merely because that platform can produce it. Missing companions matter only when the
+visible activity and collection scope require them.
+
+For macOS evidence, distinguish Unified Log exports, Endpoint Security/eslogger
+notifications, shell histories and normalized EDR records. Their fields and meanings are
+not interchangeable. Check the documented OS/message version before alleging a native
+schema defect; distinguish unfamiliar or unverified details from demonstrated errors.
+An ES notification is an observation, not necessarily an authorization decision. A plist
+write, launch-item registration, activation, process exit and unload are separate facts;
+a policy prompt does not establish denial, and a denied execution cannot prove child
+execution or its effects. Preserve the same evidence standard on every platform.
+
+Judge lifecycle relationships within the visible window. A process or session may begin
+before collection starts or survive after it ends. Flag impossible visible relationships,
+not an absent out-of-window initiating or closing record alone.
+
